fact WorkItem(id: "WI-823", description: some(value: "FIX the op-param requires coverage gap (outcome b of WI-817's witness): an op-scoped requires over the operation's OWN [T] type param (proposal 042) does not license the covered abstract spec-op call — the call is rejected at load with DispatchNoMatch — even with no recursion and no lambda. MEASURED, pinned in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs: op_param_requires_is_rejected_at_load — minimal form, operation probe[PT](x: PT) requires Desc[PT] = Desc.describe(x), rejected with (type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches), BOTH binding spellings (Desc[PT] and Desc[T = PT]) identically; op_param_control_and_witness_rejected_identically — the full mutual-recursion control and lambda witness die at the SAME site (f's covered describe call, not the recursion). The kernel spec (docs/kernel-language.md par. 5.4) explicitly admits operation type parameters in requires positions, so this is a GAP, not a rule — and it blocks the PUREST rendering of the WI-817 question, the explicit-type-argument polymorphic recursion (f[Wrap[A = GT]]). SUSPECTED MECHANISM, NOT yet established — DISCRIMINATE FIRST with a probe before fixing: either (hypothesis 1) the op-scoped requires clause over an op param is never collected into TypingEnv.op_requires (set_op_requires source — does env.op_requires() contain the entry at classification time?), or (hypothesis 2) it is collected but op_requires_covers_call's sigma walk misses the identity because an op-level type param has TWO symbols (WI-708 dual-keying, bare vs op-scoped ns.op.T) and the requires entry's element and the call's carrier resolve to different keys. A one-probe discrimination decides which layer owns the fix. DEPENDS WI-822: without the op-scoped supply channel, fixing coverage alone merely converts the (b) load rejection into the (c) unbound-dict eval error — an intermediate state that would churn the pins twice; land after WI-822 so the (b) pins flip once, to values. ACCEPTANCE: the two (b) pins FLIP and are updated — the minimal probe loads and evaluates (drive = 1), the full op-param CONTROL and LAMBDA WITNESS load clean and evaluate 1/12/122 by depth (this is the ticket that finally makes the proposal-042 spelling of the witness RUN); WI-562/member coverage behaviour unchanged for sort-param op-scoped requires; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-822"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-16T21:10:31Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-823", author: "user", content: "PRE-FIX SIGNATURE IN THIS TICKET IS NOW STALE (WI-824, delivered 2026-07-25). This ticket quotes the (b) pins as failing with 'type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches' — that is no longer what they report. WI-824 removed the bogus candidate the uncovered call used to match (an unconditioned parametric provider head matched var-to-structure), so with no candidates at all the call now lands on the WI-325 ladder: 'type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.requires: expected requires Desc[...] covering abstract type parameter, got missing requires Desc[T = ...] on enclosing sort'. Both pins were updated in place and still pin THIS gap at THIS site (f's covered describe call) — the acceptance is unchanged (they must flip to clean loads that evaluate 1/12/122), only the pre-fix text differs. Do not chase the old string.", at: "2026-07-25T05:53:52Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-823", author: "user", content: "THE GAP WAS ALREADY CLOSED, BY WI-943, AND NOT BY THIS SESSION — so what this ticket delivers is the CONFIRMATION and the missing DEFENCE, said plainly rather than claimed as a fix.\n\nATTRIBUTION. WI-943 gave a BRACKET type parameter a canonical variable of its own (`record_type_param_var` at the op-bracket site in kb/load.rs's operation conversion). Pre-fix a WRITTEN `PT` resolved only through the `SortAlias` channel, which a bracket parameter is deliberately absent from (\"an op type-param is its own logical variable\"), so `PT` resolved to NOTHING and `op_requires_covers` — which asks whether the clause's element and the call's carrier are the same variable — could not match anything. THE TICKET'S HYPOTHESIS 2 NAMED THE RIGHT LAYER AND THE WRONG CAUSE: it predicted WI-708 dual-keying (an op param having TWO symbols, bare vs op-scoped). WI-943 established there is ONE variable; the second answer was an UNRELATED sort's `T`, reached through a short-name guess — which is why `PT`/`FT`/`GT`, colliding with nothing, had no answer at all while WI-942's `T` \"worked\" by colliding with `anthill.kernel.T`. Hypothesis 1 (never collected into `TypingEnv.op_requires`) was false: the entries are collected, and the sizing probe below re-confirms they are present at the frame.\n\nACCEPTANCE VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT, not by reading test names — both (b) pins pass, having been flipped and RENAMED under WI-943 (`op_param_requires_is_licensed_by_the_operations_own_clause`, `op_param_control_and_witness_recurse_identically`): the minimal probe evaluates 1, and the op-param CONTROL and LAMBDA WITNESS both load clean and compute the depth-coded 1 / 12 / 122. The \"WI-562/member coverage unchanged for sort-param op-scoped requires\" clause is measured BOTH WAYS below, not merely observed green.\n\nWHAT WAS MISSING, AND IS WHAT THIS TICKET ADDS. The licence was defended by exactly those two fixtures, which share ONE shape: a single bracket parameter, an EXPLICIT type argument at the call site, and NO negative control anywhere — so a licence that had gone blanket, or that rode the call-site bracket, or that collapsed two parameters, would have kept both of them green. New file rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi823_op_param_requires_coverage_test.rs, 7 tests, every one driven to a VALUE on the depth-coded Desc fixture (1 / 12 / 122, so a licence granted to the WRONG dictionary yields a detectably different number rather than \"some Int\"):\n  * an_op_param_call_is_covered_with_and_without_an_explicit_type_argument — 4 cases, explicit/inferred type argument x base/conditional provider (1, 1, 12, 12). Says the identity comes from the operation's own declaration, not from the bracket at the call site — the axis both existing pins hold fixed.\n  * two_op_params_take_their_own_clauses — `pair[A, B]`, the EXACT shape WI-943 named as collapsing under the old short-name reader (A and B answered with two unrelated sorts). 112 = 100*1 + 12, so a crossed or shared dictionary lands on 1212 or on wrong digit slots, never on 112 by accident.\n  * the_clause_is_keyed_on_the_param_it_names — the discriminating PAIR, one program shape and two spellings: `requires Desc[PT]` over the parameter the call carries LOADS and gives 1; `requires Desc[QT]` over the operation's OTHER parameter is REFUSED. The refusal alone measures nothing (it is refused either way, pre-fix because no parameter had an identity at all); it earns its place as the neighbour that stops the positive half from passing on a blanket licence.\n  * an_op_param_clause_composes_with_its_sorts_own — 101, both an op-scoped and a sort-level clause covering two calls from ONE body.\n  * an_op_param_requirement_rides_a_relay_hop — 12, the instantiation carried across a hop into a second op-param operation's own clause.\n  * a_structured_op_param_element_covers_its_call — `requires Desc[T = Wrap[A = PT]]`, 12: the element need not be the bare parameter.\n  * an_op_param_call_with_no_clause_is_still_refused — refused BOTH ways BY DESIGN, and said so at its site.\n\nTHE BACK-OUT WAS RUN, NOT PREDICTED, with the recompile verified. Dropping the one line `self.kb.record_type_param_var(declared, vid)` at the op-bracket site in load.rs (the WI-943 channel in its post-WI-954 single-map form): 6 of the 7 new tests FAIL at load, the 7th being the by-design refusal; wi817's two (b) pins FAIL; wi943's three op-param tests FAIL. AND EVERY SORT-PARAMETER TEST STAYS GREEN — wi817's op_scoped_sort_param_simple_concrete_works, op_scoped_single_conditional_level_is_correct, op_scoped_recursion_correct_control_and_lambda_identical, op_scoped_relay_chain_correct_via_value_direction, sort_level_* and sort_param_applier_witness_matches_control; wi943's sort_type_param_resolves_through_its_sort_alias. That green half is the ticket's \"unchanged for sort-param op-scoped requires\" clause measured from both sides, and it is also what makes the back-out a back-out of the OP-PARAM fix specifically rather than of type parameters generally. The back-out prose is recorded in the new file's header so a later reader can re-run it.\n\n/code-review (high): 3 FINDINGS, ALL FIXED, and the first was a real defect in this ticket's own test. (1) MEDIUM — an_op_param_clause_composes_with_its_sorts_own drove `probe[Leaf](leaf(), leaf())`, so BOTH calls coded 1 and the asserted 101 = 100*1 + 1 was produced just as well by a body that served each call from the OTHER clause. The doc comment claimed the value would catch that crossing; it could not. It now drives `(leaf(), wrap(leaf()))` for 112 = 100*1 + 12, where a crossed pair gives 1201 — and the back-out was RE-MEASURED on the changed file (still 6 of 7 failing, the 7th the by-design refusal), because the driver changed after the first measurement. The mistake is recorded at the test's own site rather than silently corrected. (2) LOW — both refusal assertions matched the message TEXT but not the SITE, and DESC_INSTANCES contains its OWN abstract `Desc.describe(w.inner)` call in `WrapDesc.describe` under a sort-level licence, so a regression THERE would have raised the same wording and kept both refusals green while witnessing nothing about op-param keying. They now pin `<ns>.Desc.describe.requires` as well, through a named `assert_refused_at` that says why. (3) LOW/reuse — this was the FOURTH byte-identical copy of `const MISSING_REQUIRES`, the same trigger and the same count at which DESC_INSTANCES itself was lifted; moved to `common::MISSING_DESC_REQUIRES` beside it, with wi817/wi822/wi824 repointed. The literal is producer-coupled to the `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` format! in kb/typing.rs, so a reworded diagnostic now desyncs one reader instead of four.\n\nRESIDUE, recorded as FEEDBACK ON WI-817 (its open parent) rather than as a new ticket, because WI-823 is itself a follow-up and CLAUDE.md forbids a follow-up on a follow-up: the DOT spelling and the NAMED spelling disagree on a `requires`-constrained type-parameter receiver — `x.describe()` is refused at load where `Desc.describe(x)` loads and evaluates, identically for all three `requires` spellings and unchanged by the WI-943 back-out, while both a concrete receiver and an abstract-spec receiver agree across the two spellings. Not inline-sized, and that was MEASURED at the frame rather than assumed: `env.op_requires()` holds the constraining entry for the two op-scoped shapes but is EMPTY for the sort-level one, so a fix needs two chain sources, a sigma-alignment against the receiver's type variable, an ambiguity rule, and a par. 3538 decision the spec has not made. Full detail in WI-817's feedback.", at: "2026-08-16T21:10:29Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-823", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the delivery note above, at the user's direction: the residue is now WI-1119, not WI-817 feedback. The delivery text says the dot-spelling finding was 'recorded as FEEDBACK ON WI-817 (its open parent, not a new ticket)'; the user asked for it as its own ticket, so the WI-817 feedback row was removed and the finding moved verbatim into WI-1119 (Open, no dependencies), which carries the full measurement, the par. 3538 decision it needs, the two working controls, the instrumented mechanism and an acceptance that covers BOTH possible decisions. Nothing else in the delivery note changes.", at: "2026-08-16T21:23:19Z")
